{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-213056",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we decompose the exponent into a product of smaller exponents. For example,\n\n$$\n{12}^{6}=(3\\cdot 4)^{6}={3}^{3}\\cdot {3}^{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNext, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3} & ≡27\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡3\\cdot 9+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡3\\cdot 0+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\ {4}^{3} & ≡64\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡7\\cdot 9+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡7\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{12}^{6} & ≡{3}^{3}\\cdot {3}^{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡0\\cdot 0\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{12}^{6}≡0\\,\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9).\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
